# SYNOPSIS

```text
userboot.next=bin/launcher launcher.until=audio-test
```

# DESCRIPTION

audio-test is the last service of the audio image, `bootfs-audio-test.img`, which holds devmgr(8) with its drivers and the services its manifest mounts.
The boot-audio test boots it in QEMU with a virtio-sound-pci, an output and an input stream, through the IOMMU, on QEMU's silent backend (`-audiodev none`), which takes and gives frames at the device's rate.
The launcher runs until audio-test ends and exits with its code (launcher(8)).

It waits up to 30 seconds for virtio-snd(3) to bring the device up or fail, and prints the driver's `status`, a line each, `audio-test: status: <line>`.
It checks that the driver found an output and an input stream with no control request failed, that devmgr binds the device at `/dev/audio/0` with `ctl`, `stat`, `volume` and `data`, that `ctl` starts in 9front's format, and that `stat` names the device and what each stream takes and starts with 9front's audiostat line; it prints that `stat`.
It writes half a second of a 440 Hz tone, 16-bit stereo at 44,100 frames a second, to `data` in 8 KiB writes, waits up to 10 seconds for `stat` to say the stream stopped, and checks that it drained by itself after one run, that the device played a period for every 1024 frames written, that `stat`'s offset counts the bytes, and that playing took at least 0.4 seconds, the device taking frames at its rate.
It reads a quarter of a second from `data`, checks that it got it, stops capture through `ctl` and checks that `stat` says so.
Then it sets `speed 48000`, checks that `ctl` reads it back and refuses `speed 12345`, and plays a tenth of a second, which must run and drain again.
A line for each check is `audio-test: ok:` or `audio-test: FAILED:` and what it checks; its last line is `audio-test: passed` or `audio-test: <n> failed`.
It exits 0 if every check passed.

